60f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" "q-117369/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" "q-117369/selection/1e5815fe88f764dcffb6e1d4884bd91ce2c23cb2e4c57c2f2f99b70378aa1f23" "q-117369/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" "q-117369/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-117369" :question/id #uuid "d42d29a1-7ec6-555e-8ef7-3216eb563375" :question/math-academy-id "q-117369" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x?$\n\n- $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n- $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First,  we consider the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,-\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\sqrt{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-117369/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-117369"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-65991/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "7099c591-d7b1-5c3a-bd28-9be160343555" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65991/selection/b1a9f2bb9f9bd59b66c906c9ab98446a26ec623744a7f92356bb121785f6cea5" :answer/id #uuid "24d64247-0205-543f-a0e2-490f1d6b3427" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65991/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "c81354cf-bbf2-5438-989f-32c0b0c737c9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65991/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "040c344e-d37a-5421-a555-f62c67aa0343" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65991/selection/6a87b4860f595a380edef797b2cc43b24d4896fc51da713259b980588721456c" :answer/id #uuid "7fdec069-17cf-5c8a-8919-d967b715eb13" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-65991/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "49936ff6-5563-5dcd-88e6-ff051bc9ea7b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answ