 range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=0$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,0)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{3},-1)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-65991/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-65991"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-70789/selection/95f63c11f99a5015c1f73558576052145e00c1cfbb1718f78cf554be0ab200f9" :answer/id #uuid "c4ac8f12-61df-539b-8840-06aff6c37637" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70789/selection/b1a9f2bb9f9bd59b66c906c9ab98446a26ec623744a7f92356bb121785f6cea5" :answer/id #uuid "2b523dc2-8e02-5ed8-9acf-155303f63c61" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70789/selection/5b26512916f768e66a2b0af106e5aa3e2ff7ee012277d9669a26d349c343f77b" :answer/id #uuid "70efbf46-9339-58e3-8485-f574308b5975" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70789/selection/1f1f2fe9df6485d5cdc7916416b2f79699d874348406e639d5daec02cb2e57e6" :answer/id #uuid "8ce96561-60c6-5e82-9852-c02021edb9d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-70789/selection/df67a18079e3b7b726c109ae1bf07ae161d9f6d37a928c50361c8787d74f7981" :answer/id #uuid "78432abd-fcb3-599b-af22-c10884f5f6f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db