y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},0)$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(0,\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x$ is $y\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ does **not** lie within this range. $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-65979/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f045b43-4785-5989-8b5a-dcb8401d1bb3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-65979"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
